{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>A stranger and a settler, etc.</b> The reason for his argument of ״a stranger and a settler״ is based on what Rambam wrote in chapter 3 of the Laws of Acquisition and Gifts, and this is his language: one may give a gift without consideration to a ger toshav whom you are commanded to sustain, as it is written (Leviticus 25:35) ״a stranger and a settler and he shall live with you״ — end quote. And you should know that all of our holy Torah is rational, and particularly in matters of worldly conduct; just as we are duty-bound in our conduct toward a ger dwelling among us, so too the rational obligation of the inhabitants of the earth requires them to conduct themselves among one another to sustain a person who is a stranger and a settler among them and to give him a gift without consideration — and this is the argument of Abraham: <b>a stranger and a settler am I; give me.</b> And he was precise in saying ״a stranger״ and did not suffice with saying ״a settler״ — the intent being to say that even though I am a stranger and not one of you, nevertheless I am a settler. Further, the reason he said <b>a stranger</b> is that he was careful not to say of himself that he is a settler in this world, for that is the opposite of the trait of the righteous; therefore he prefaced by saying ״a stranger.״" "<b>And I will bury, etc.</b> — meaning, that they should not detain him. Further, he intended to say: since he is about to reveal his intent that he desires the field of Ephron and will give money for it, they will say: what did Abraham see to give all this for a mere burial plot in the ground? It is certain that he saw in it something great, worth many times over! Therefore he prefaced by saying ״and I will bury my dead״ — to bury it from before me, meaning, this is what compels him not to be concerned about his money, and if his dead were not before him he would not acquire anything. And from now on, even if he purchases the field of Ephron with all the money in the world, they will conclude that it is a choice place for burial, and to them there is nothing [remarkable] in it."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:52.784975002Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}